00:01:45.703 --> 00:01:49.343
Hanover re-reported a 21.1% return on equity

00:01:49.343 --> 00:01:52.223
in its latest quarter, on strong profitability.

00:01:52.723 --> 00:01:57.643
A print investing dot com carried as the case that a reinsure can compound capital when

00:01:57.643 --> 00:01:59.123
risk selection stays tight.

00:01:59.843 --> 00:02:04.483
Valuation investing in the form we are testing is the decision to buy that compounding

00:02:04.483 --> 00:02:07.403
when the market is still paying less than the balance sheet implies.

00:02:07.943 --> 00:02:11.003
Then hold until the gap closes or the underwriting breaks.

00:02:11.563 --> 00:02:16.183
The firm got there by keeping combined ratios in check while premium volume still grew,

00:02:16.183 --> 00:02:21.103
which is the insurance version of a restaurant checking whether food and labor costs less than the

00:02:21.103 --> 00:02:26.523
menu price. If the kitchen spends more than it charges, no amount of busy tables saves the year.

00:02:27.123 --> 00:02:33.103
You see a name with return on equity above 21% and a price to book sitting under its own five-year

00:02:33.103 --> 00:02:38.343
average. Do you buy the discount now or do you wait for the next catastrophe season to prove the

00:02:38.343 --> 00:02:43.923
reserves? Here's how I'd think about it. Run the broker-fundamental scanner for return on equity

00:02:43.923 --> 00:02:49.903
above 15% and a stable combined ratio, then stack the current price to book against that

00:02:49.903 --> 00:02:53.083
5-year average so the discount is a number, not a feeling.

00:02:53.743 --> 00:02:58.503
That screen earned its keep in earlier rate cycle peaks, when excess capital went into

00:02:58.503 --> 00:03:00.123
buybacks instead of new risk.

00:03:00.523 --> 00:03:05.703
It failed when catastrophe losses outran reserves and return on equity spent multiple quarters

00:03:05.703 --> 00:03:06.963
under 10%.

00:03:06.963 --> 00:03:13.303
Park the screen inside a TFSA so any re-rating is deferred, and watch quarterly reserve releases

00:03:13.303 --> 00:03:15.923
is the way you'd watch that kitchen's food cost line.

00:03:16.723 --> 00:03:19.923
Deterioration shows up there before it shows up in the headline yield.

00:03:20.483 --> 00:03:24.223
Canadian accounts can run the same filters on TSX listed insurers

00:03:24.623 --> 00:03:27.923
and skip the currency conversion, which is the quieter edge

00:03:27.923 --> 00:03:30.963
if the US names are only cheap after the FX haircut.

00:03:32.023 --> 00:03:35.303
Essent Group, ticker ESNT on the New York Stock Exchange,

00:03:35.683 --> 00:03:38.783
or NYSE, is the simulated weekly hold,

00:03:39.243 --> 00:03:42.423
a motley full noted valuation entry on excess capital

00:03:42.423 --> 00:03:44.623
that can take share when competitors retrench.

00:03:45.083 --> 00:03:47.663
This is a simulated trade for learning purposes.

00:03:48.123 --> 00:03:51.003
$1,000, five sessions from the next open,

00:03:51.443 --> 00:03:53.843
scored against the NASDAQ over that same window,

00:03:54.263 --> 00:03:56.783
and it is the valuation investing screen applied

00:03:56.783 --> 00:03:59.383
to a mortgage insurer rather than a momentum chase.

00:04:00.063 --> 00:04:02.603
The catalyst is a reinsurance tail risk shield

00:04:02.603 --> 00:04:05.803
plus surplus capital, which lets essence keep writing

00:04:05.803 --> 00:04:07.823
when pure mortgage insurers pull back.

00:04:08.343 --> 00:04:10.683
And that flexibility has historically shown up

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:14.343
as basis points of new insurance written in housing slowdowns.

00:04:14.383 --> 00:04:19.243
The technical read is priced a book under its historical average with volume stable versus

00:04:19.243 --> 00:04:20.183
the 20-day mean.

00:04:20.743 --> 00:04:22.183
Not a spike you have to chase.

00:04:22.863 --> 00:04:27.103
A name with that capital cushion and a cheap book, with the next earnings print still

00:04:27.103 --> 00:04:31.783
a quarter away, do you take the five-session hold or wait for the combined ratio number?

00:04:32.303 --> 00:04:36.863
I take the hold only after the metric edge is on the ticket, and I name the kill switch

00:04:36.863 --> 00:04:43.783
first, the stop sits 8% below entry, which caps the simulated loss at $80 if it tags intraday.

00:04:44.223 --> 00:04:49.883
The target band is 2% to 5%, and confidence is medium because capital strength and the

00:04:49.883 --> 00:04:54.903
rotation into insurers line up while earnings visibility remains 1-quarter out.

00:04:55.443 --> 00:05:01.263
If the next reported combined ratio prints above 85%, the thesis is finished on that release.

00:05:01.863 --> 00:05:04.363
We do not wait for price to argue with the underwriting.

00:05:05.123 --> 00:05:09.563
Valuation discipline and a stop name before the money is at risk are the two rules that

00:05:09.563 --> 00:05:10.543
put this on the pad.

00:05:10.963 --> 00:05:16.303
We size after the balance sheet edge is confirmed, and the reinsurance layer is the downside

00:05:16.303 --> 00:05:18.063
the pure writers do not have.

00:05:18.663 --> 00:05:24.543
POW was the valuation and insurance earnings hold from about 14 days back, and that name

00:05:24.543 --> 00:05:27.063
is up 3.97% since the pick.

00:05:27.663 --> 00:05:32.523
That is a working tape on the same family, not a matched window score, and I will not

00:05:32.523 --> 00:05:36.223
not pretended as alpha without the Nasdaq sitting beside it for those sessions.

00:05:36.783 --> 00:05:42.203
The original thesis was capital and earnings growth, not a chart break, which is why today's

00:05:42.203 --> 00:05:46.183
essence ticket leans on the same discipline instead of on a hot sector tape.

00:05:46.743 --> 00:05:51.943
The invalidation on that earlier idea was fundamental deterioration, and nothing in this tape

00:05:51.943 --> 00:05:53.203
says that trigger fired.

00:05:53.723 --> 00:05:55.983
The percentage is still a result, not a method.

00:05:56.543 --> 00:06:01.643
I am not exiting that name on feel, because discretionary cuts are exactly what the five

00:06:01.643 --> 00:06:06.603
session rule was built to stop. If this one had gone the other way, I would treat the first

00:06:06.603 --> 00:06:12.423
tag stop as the cheap loss and study whether the book discount was real or just a sector drawdown

00:06:12.423 --> 00:06:18.483
wearing a value mask. Insurance rotation is the shared backdrop, so the live comparison is whether

00:06:18.483 --> 00:06:23.763
excess capital still shows up in the next underwriting print. Not whether the last ticker made us

00:06:23.763 --> 00:06:29.826
feel clever. Interactive broker's option chains will surface covered call premiums with annualized

00:06:29.826 --> 00:06:32.646
yields above 15% on 30-day expires.

00:06:33.306 --> 00:06:34.986
And I have no financial relationship

00:06:34.986 --> 00:06:37.966
with interactive brokers or any other platform name today.

00:06:38.706 --> 00:06:40.966
Login, select the underlying and filter

00:06:40.966 --> 00:06:43.386
for open interest above 500 contracts,

00:06:43.826 --> 00:06:45.866
so the quote you see can actually be hit.

00:06:46.426 --> 00:06:49.586
That screen is how you price the income on a flat RRSP

00:06:49.586 --> 00:06:52.306
holding without guessing whether the call is worth writing.

00:06:53.006 --> 00:06:55.246
Export the premium table into a spreadsheet

00:06:55.246 --> 00:06:57.746
and line up annualized yields across names

00:06:57.746 --> 00:07:03.106
before you choose which book to overwrite. Because opportunity cost is a column, not a vibe.

00:07:03.786 --> 00:07:08.606
Use next to today's valuation hold, the chain tells you what you would be paid to cap upside

00:07:08.606 --> 00:07:13.666
on a name you already decided is cheap, which is a different decision from the five-session share

00:07:13.666 --> 00:07:21.836
ticket. Bitcoin, investing.com notes, slid to $74,000. It's lowest since mid-August as ETF

00:07:21.836 --> 00:07:25.196
for Demtians accelerated and policy clarity stayed missing.

